[META]
framework: claude-framework
file_id: backlog.OPERATIONS
class: universal
version: 1.0.0

[OPERATIONS]
overview: The five operations the agent performs on the backlog: add, list, show, update, activate, archive
principle: Every operation reads INDEX.toon first. Detail files are touched only when the operation requires detail.

[OP_ADD]
purpose: Capture a new item
inputs: title (required), type (required), priority (default P2), theme (optional), motivation + description (for detail), source (who triggered: user / spawn_task / workflow / ...)
steps:
  1. Read INDEX.toon → find `next_id_seq` → new id = `BL-<zero-padded seq>`
  2. Append [ITEM.<id>] block (7 lines) to [ITEMS] section
  3. Re-sort [ITEMS] (see SCHEMA.toon order rule)
  4. Increment `next_id_seq`
  5. Update `last_updated`
  6. Write items/<id>.toon with the full detail schema
  7. Write initial HISTORY line
  8. Respond: "Captured as <id>" + one-line recap
duration_budget: Under 5 tool calls (read INDEX, write INDEX, write detail = 3 minimum)

[OP_LIST]
purpose: Show what's in the backlog, filtered
inputs: filter (optional: status, type, theme, priority)
steps:
  1. Read INDEX.toon only (NEVER read detail files for listing)
  2. Filter entries by the requested criteria
  3. Present as a compact table: id | title | type | status | priority | theme
response_budget: Keep under 25 rows in the shown output; if more match, paginate or tighten filter

[OP_SHOW]
purpose: Display full detail of one item
inputs: id
steps:
  1. Read items/<id>.toon
  2. Present DESCRIPTION, MOTIVATION, ACCEPTANCE, OUT_OF_SCOPE, LINKS, HISTORY (last 5 entries)
  3. Do NOT re-read INDEX unless detail fields conflict with INDEX — then run INTEGRITY_RULES repair from SCHEMA.toon

[OP_UPDATE]
purpose: Change fields of an item (title, priority, theme, description, acceptance, out_of_scope, links)
inputs: id, fields to change
NOT_this_op: Status transitions — those go through OP_TRANSITION (see LIFECYCLE.toon)
steps:
  1. Read items/<id>.toon
  2. Apply changes
  3. If change touches a shared field (title/type/priority/theme) → also update INDEX.toon entry
  4. Append HISTORY line: "<ISO> — updated: <fields changed> — <who: user/agent>"
  5. Update INDEX `last_updated`

[OP_TRANSITION]
purpose: Change status (see LIFECYCLE.toon transition matrix)
inputs: id, new status, reason
gate: If transition requires user (per LIFECYCLE matrix), STOP and ask. Do not proceed without explicit "yes".
steps:
  1. Verify legality against LIFECYCLE transition matrix
  2. If requires approval and not yet granted → propose and wait
  3. Update INDEX [ITEM.<id>].status
  4. Update items/<id>.toon [ITEM].status
  5. Append HISTORY line
  6. Re-sort INDEX [ITEMS] by status order
  7. Update INDEX `last_updated`

[OP_ACTIVATE]
purpose: Start actual work on an item — bridge to the workflow module
inputs: id
preconditions:
  - Item status is `accepted` (preferred) or `proposed` (with user approval) or `deferred`
  - In-progress concurrency cap not exceeded (see LIFECYCLE.toon)
steps:
  1. Read items/<id>.toon in full
  2. If status is `proposed` and user hasn't accepted — ask: "BL-<id> is still `proposed`. Accept and activate? [y/n]"
  3. Run OP_TRANSITION to in-progress
  4. Start workflow module (see docs/agent/workflow/WORKFLOW.toon):
     - Task classification comes from [ITEM].type (feature → FEATURE.toon, refactor → REFACTOR.toon, bugfix → BUGFIX.toon, chore/docs → plain PHASES)
     - DISCOVER phase is pre-seeded with DESCRIPTION + MOTIVATION + ACCEPTANCE + OUT_OF_SCOPE from the detail file
     - PLAN phase's "Out of scope" section inherits from item's OUT_OF_SCOPE
     - PLAN phase's verification items inherit from item's ACCEPTANCE
  5. Create workplan at .claude/workplan/<slug>.md; slug is derived from item title (see workflow/STATE_TRACKING.toon)
  6. Set items/<id>.toon [LINKS].workplan = <slug>
  7. Run workflow normally. On REPORT success → auto-transition in-progress → done; auto-archive the workplan.

[OP_ARCHIVE]
purpose: Move old terminal items out of the hot INDEX
inputs: none (scans automatically) or specific id
trigger: Manual (user says "archive the backlog") OR automatic when [ITEMS] exceeds 80 entries
steps:
  1. Identify candidates: status in {done, rejected} AND last HISTORY entry older than 30 days
  2. Ensure archive/INDEX.toon exists (create if needed, same schema)
  3. For each candidate:
     - Move entry from INDEX.toon [ITEMS] to archive/INDEX.toon [ITEMS]
     - Move items/<id>.toon to items/archive/<id>.toon (or leave in place — config TBD; default: leave items/ files untouched)
  4. Update both INDEX files' `last_updated`
note: Archived items remain readable via OP_SHOW; OP_LIST by default excludes archive — `--include-archive` to include

[OP_SPAWN_FROM_WORKFLOW]
purpose: Specific entry point — workflow phases auto-capture follow-ups as backlog items
triggered_from:
  - REFACTOR.toon RULE_5 ("scope discipline"): out-of-scope items noted mid-refactor
  - BUGFIX.toon RULE_5 ("no opportunistic changes"): adjacent issues spotted but not fixed
  - FEATURE.toon RULE_7 ("no future proofing"): ideas that came up but were deferred
  - PHASES.toon Phase 4 ("while I'm here" notes)
flow:
  1. Workflow context generates a short note ("scope drift spotted: X could be simplified")
  2. Agent calls OP_ADD with:
     - title = note summary
     - type = inferred (refactor / bugfix / chore)
     - priority = P3 default (user can bump later)
     - source = "workflow.<phase>.<rule>"
  3. Agent mentions in the ongoing task's REPORT: "Captured BL-<id> as follow-up"

[MUTATIONS_AND_CONCURRENCY]
- All mutations go through OPERATIONS; agent never edits INDEX.toon or detail files by free-form write
- Always read-modify-write INDEX in a single logical step; don't interleave with other operations
- If two operations in one turn both mutate INDEX → batch the changes, write INDEX once at the end
